This dynamic becomes acute during high-volatility events. The Mexico versus England advancement market currently shows approximately forty-seven cents on Mexico and fifty-three cents on England — a tight spread reflecting genuine uncertainty. A trader who bet heavily on one side with most of their account faces potential fifty-percent-plus drawdown on resolution. Without remaining capital to deploy elsewhere, they're effectively leveraged to a single binary outcome.

The Zelenskyy leadership market illustrates another dimension of Polymarket leverage risk: tail events with extreme pricing. Shares trade at fractions of a cent, meaning the implied leverage on a YES outcome is enormous. Traders who load up on these extreme longshots are taking positions where small absolute losses represent total capital destruction if sized improperly.

## What Smart Money Does Differently

When a [convergence signal](/what-is-a-convergence-signal/) emerges from multiple verified profitable traders taking the same position, one consistent pattern appears: conservative sizing relative to account equity. The signal isn't just about direction — it's about how much capital experienced traders commit.

